--TEST--
A Core\Ast\Node's kind names the same construct as that node's own runtime class, at every node of a walk — `rule:core-classes/ast-is-inert`, `rule:core-api/reference-card`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: Core\Ast\Node::kind
// The *agreement* shape, over every node of one tree. `kind()` is the short
// name a program branches on and the node's runtime class is the identity
// behind it, so the two name the same construct at every node or one of them
// is wrong. The assertion counts rather than reading a line off the walk, so a
// tree answering plausibly node by node still fails here — and the count is
// named too, so a walk that agreed by visiting nothing fails as well.
var $src = <<<'NVS'
<?nvs
int $count = 2;
if ($count > 1) {
    foreach ([1, 2] as int $n) {
        echo $n;
    }
}
NVS;
var $file = Core\Ast::parse($src);

uint $checked = 0;
uint $agreed = 0;
foreach ($file->nodes() as Core\Ast\Node $node) {
    $checked = $checked + 1;
    if (Core\Reflect::forObject($node)->name() == "Core\\Ast\\" . $node->kind()) {
        $agreed = $agreed + 1;
    }
}

echo "checked ", $checked, " agreed ", $agreed, "\n";
echo $checked == $agreed ? "every node agrees" : "a node named two constructs", "\n";

// The root is a node too, and it is not part of its own walk.
echo $file->kind(), " ", Core\Reflect::forObject($file)->name(), "\n";

// The name is the grammar's, not the source's: these parts are written with
// construct names in them and answer what they are.
foreach (Core\Ast::parse('<?nvs string $ClassDecl = "Foreach"; echo "File";')->children() as Core\Ast\Node $part) {
    echo $part->kind(), " ";
}
echo "\n";
--EXPECT--
checked 14 agreed 14
every node agrees
File Core\Ast\File
LocalDecl Echo
